The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2014 (2011), 2051 (2012), 2140 (2014), 2201 (2015), 2204 (2015), 2216 (2015), 2266 (2016), 2342 (2017) and the statements of its President concerning Yemen,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the unity, sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of Yemen,

Expressing its grave concern at the continued deterioration of the devastating humanitarian situation in Yemen and the tragic humanitarian consequences of the conflict for the people of Yemen, and emphasizing the necessity of facilitating rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access,

Expressing grave concern also at the ongoing violence in Yemen and the threats to the stability and security of the region, including the continued attacks by the Houthis on the territory of Saudi Arabia, in particular through the launch of ballistic missiles,

Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts on Yemen (S/2018/68),

Expressing particular concern that the Panel of Experts identified remnants of missiles related to military equipment of Iranian origin that were introduced into Yemen after the implementation of the targeted arms embargo, and that the Islamic Republic of Iran is in non-compliance with paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015) for failing to take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer to designated individuals and entities of such items,

Determining that the situation in Yemen continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 26 February 2019 the measures imposed by paragraphs 11 and 15 of resolution 2140 (2014), re-emphasizes the importance of the implementation of the targeted arms embargo imposed by paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), and reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 12, 13, 14 and 16 of resolution 2140 (2014) and of paragraph 20 of resolution 2216 (2015);

2. Decides to extend until 28 March 2019 the mandate of the Panel of Experts as set out in paragraph 21 of resolution 2140 (2014), and paragraph 21 of resolution 2216 (2015), expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding the further extension no later than 28 February 2019, and requests the Secretary-General to take the necessary administrative measures as expeditiously as possible to re-establish the Panel of Experts;

3. Condemns in the strongest terms the ballistic missile attacks launched by the Houthis against civilian and military targets in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, and demands that the Houthis cease all such attacks immediately;

4. Expresses its particular concern that the Panel of Experts has identified that ballistic missiles and other military equipment from the Islamic Republic of Iran were introduced into Yemen after the imposition of the targeted arms embargo, resulting in non-compliance with paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), and expresses its intention to consider additional measures to address non-compliance with the targeted arms embargo;

5. Urges all parties to facilitate the rapid and unhindered passage of humanitarian relief for all civilians in need across the country, as well as rapid and unhindered commercial access, in particular through all of Yemen’s ports and Sana’a airport, consistent with international humanitarian law;

6. Urges all parties to resume peaceful consultations without preconditions and in good faith with the United Nations Special Envoy for Yemen towards a comprehensive and inclusive political agreement;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

